\section*{QMA hardness of continuum electronic energy with binary nuclear charges}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents a proof in the selected manuscript. Approximating full continuum electronic Coulomb energy is claimed QMA-hard with binary-encoded positive nuclear charges, rational distinct nuclei, and unary electron count.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
A finite local-Hamiltonian instance is converted to positive spin interactions through controllable contact geometry. Coulomb orbital and exchange estimates realize that interaction in the antisymmetric continuum, with binary charge scaling preserving polynomial description length and a unit energy separation.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
Approximating full continuum electronic Coulomb energy is claimed QMA-hard with binary-encoded positive nuclear charges, rational distinct nuclei, and unary electron count. The question retains these same parameters and conventions. Within these assumptions, this is a claimed affirmative answer to the stated question.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 275, the documented Comparator contains deterministic polynomial reductions for both binary nuclear charges and unit charges in the full antisymmetric spinful continuum with unit threshold separation. The listed Comparator files are \texttt{ContinuumCoulombHardness.lean}. The inventory does not supply a named declaration for these Comparator entries. This site has not executed the Lean code. The scope descriptions do not certify the remaining manuscript arguments.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these manuscripts to work by an internal model. Its release cautions that arguments without formalization can contain mistakes. This catalogue has not independently checked the proofs; the labels report the mathematical claims attributed to OpenAI.

